Abstract

Large language models (LLMs) generate text by maximizing likelihood, a surrogate utterly untethered from truth. The resulting outputs are stylistically fluent but frequently factually incorrect or logically unsound. Recent work has attempted to address this via post-hoc verification, self-refinement, or conformal prediction, but these approaches either lack rigorous coverage guarantees, assume a single static verifier, or treat uncertainty heuristically. This dissertation introduces Conformal Epistemic Processes (CEP), a mathematical framework that decouples truth-seeking from the base language model, provides finite-sample and anytime-valid coverage guarantees for iterative verification, quantifies epistemic uncertainty with proper scoring rules, and optimally controls the verification process under budget constraints.

The framework is built on five pillars:
(i) a verifiability process formulation that uses online conformal prediction with e-values to give anytime-valid false discovery rate (FDR) control, guaranteeing that accepted responses meet a verifiability threshold irrespective of the generator’s likelihood;
(ii) a kernel-based semantic variance that serves as a proper epistemic risk estimator for verifiability, bounding the epistemic uncertainty about whether a response is correct;
(iii) an optimal stopping formulation where the verification loop is cast as a restless bandit, and we prove that a threshold policy is optimal under diminishing marginal returns of verification; we provide an online learning algorithm (MPHC-UCB) with sublinear regret and a matching lower bound;
(iv) adversarially robust verifier aggregation via e-values, allowing the combination of multiple possibly colluding verifiers while maintaining anytime-valid error control; and
(v) human-criticality policies that optimally route uncertain cases to human verifiers using a Neyman-Pearson-type selective prediction framework with finite-sample risk control.

The dissertation culminates in an information-theoretic analysis that proves a fundamental lower bound on the number of verification steps required to achieve a target coverage, and we show that our adaptive policy attains this bound up to logarithmic factors—i.e., it is rate-optimal. The theoretical results are validated in simulations with both toy models and real-world LLM deployments.

Collectively, this thesis establishes a rigorous statistical foundation for verification-grounded language generation, enabling LLM systems that not only sound plausible but provably meet verifiability standards, even under adversarial conditions and limited computational budgets.

Chapter 0 — Introduction

Large language models have achieved remarkable fluency across a wide range of natural language tasks. Yet their core objective—maximizing the probability of observed token sequences—is orthogonal to truth. A model can assign high likelihood to a statement that is factually wrong, logically inconsistent, or physically impossible, while assigning low likelihood to a correct but rare answer. This misalignment between likelihood and verifiability is the fundamental bottleneck preventing LLMs from being trusted in high-stakes domains such as medicine, law, or scientific research.

Recent years have seen a surge of interest in post-hoc verification and self-refinement: after generating a candidate response, the model or an external tool checks it and possibly triggers a new generation (Gou et al., 2023; Madaan et al., 2024; Lightman et al., 2024). These approaches, while empirically promising, lack rigorous guarantees. The verification score is often a heuristic combination of LLM-as-a-judge outputs, symbolic checks, or retrieval-based signals; there is no finite-sample coverage statement—one cannot say "with 95% probability, the proportion of verified correct answers among those we accept is at least 90%." Moreover, the iterative loop is managed by ad-hoc heuristics (e.g., "verify three times then stop"), and the human-in-the-loop component is rarely, if ever, governed by a principled risk budget.

On the other hand, conformal prediction (Vovk et al., 2005) and its recent extensions to language models (Angelopoulos et al., 2024) provide distribution-free, finite-sample coverage guarantees for prediction sets. Meanwhile, the theory of e-values (Vovk & Wang, 2021; Ramdas et al., 2023) has revolutionized sequential hypothesis testing, enabling anytime-valid error control under arbitrary dependencies—precisely the kind of guarantee needed when verification proceeds step-by-step with feedback loops. In parallel, the optimal stopping and bandit literature (Whittle, 1988; Gittins, 1979) offers a rich toolkit for controlling sequential acquisition of information under costs.

This dissertation weaves these threads into a single, cohesive framework: Conformal Epistemic Processes (CEP). The central object is a verifiability process, a sequence of generated responses and oracle verifier feedbacks, equipped with a dynamic conformal testing procedure that decides whether the current response is sufficiently verified or requires further iteration. The key insight is that we can decouple coverage from the base model's likelihood—the guarantee  \mathbb{P}(V \ge 1-\epsilon) \ge 1-\alpha  holds for any generator, any prompt, and any underlying data distribution, provided only a weak exchangeability condition between a calibration set and the evaluation environment.

The dissertation makes the following contributions:

1. Sequential Conformal Coverage (Chapter 1). We formulate the verifiability process and provide an anytime-valid FDR control procedure using conformal p-values converted to e-values, processed by the e-BH algorithm. We prove that with high probability, the FDR of rejected (flagged as potentially incorrect) responses never exceeds \alpha, and consequently the accepted responses enjoy a selective coverage guarantee. We also characterize the calibration sample complexity needed for near-optimal risk control.
2. Semantic Epistemic Uncertainty (Chapter 2). We define a kernel-based semantic variance and show, under a mild mean-square Lipschitz assumption on the oracle verifiability function, that this variance is a proper scoring rule for the epistemic uncertainty about verification. This provides a rigorous, computable measure of "how uncertain is the model about whether this answer will be verified?"—distinct from prior work on answer uncertainty. Finite-sample concentration bounds are given.
3. Optimal Control of Verification (Chapter 3). We model the iterative generation-verification loop as a Markov decision process with a continuum of stopping states. We prove that under diminishing marginal returns to verification, the optimal policy is indexable and takes a threshold form. We propose MPHC-UCB, an online algorithm that learns the optimal threshold from calibration data with \tilde{O}(\sqrt{K}) regret over K tasks, and we provide a matching lower bound. This is the first regret-minimizing verification policy for language generation.
4. Adversarially Robust Verifier Aggregation (Chapter 4). When multiple verifiers (e.g., separate LLMs, retrievers, symbolic solvers) are available, their outputs may be arbitrarily dependent or even adversarial. We show that converting each verifier’s output into an e-value and multiplying them yields an anytime-valid error control procedure that maintains coverage even if verifiers collude. This is a significant robustness result, enabling safe use of LLM-based verifiers in the loop.
5. Human-Criticality as Selective Verification (Chapter 5). We frame the decision to escalate to a human as a Neyman-Pearson test based on the epistemic uncertainty U. Under a monotone likelihood ratio assumption, a threshold policy on U is optimal, and we provide finite-sample risk control using learn-then-test. This gives a rigorous, budget-aware human-in-the-loop strategy.
6. Information-Theoretic Limits (Chapter 6). Finally, we model the verifier as a noisy channel and prove a converse bound: any procedure achieving coverage 1-\alpha must use at least \Theta(H(R|C)/C) verification steps, where C is the verifier channel capacity. We provide a constructive algorithm that achieves this bound, and show that MPHC is rate-optimal.

The theoretical framework is accompanied by experiments on toy models (where closed-form solutions exist) and on real-world LLMs with tool-based verifiers, demonstrating the practical viability of the approach.

Related work and positioning. Our work intersects conformal inference (Angelopoulos et al., 2024; Gibbs & Candès, 2023), e-values and anytime valid inference (Ramdas et al., 2023), optimal stopping (Whittle, 1988), epistemic uncertainty in LMs (Kuhn et al., 2023; Wang & Holmes, 2024), and multi-agent verification (Lifshitz et al., 2025). Prior conformal LM methods provide static coverage for a single generation; we extend to iterative, multi-verifier, and budgeted settings. Prior uncertainty quantification works measure answer uncertainty; we target verification uncertainty, which is the appropriate target for downstream decision-making. The restless bandit formulation is new in LLM verification, and the information-theoretic capacity analysis is entirely novel.

Thesis structure. The remainder of this document proceeds chapter by chapter as outlined above. Each chapter is self-contained with its own proofs, while a unified notation is maintained throughout. The appendices collect detailed proofs, prior art comparisons, and algorithmic pseudo-code.

By the end of this dissertation, we aim to have established CEP as a principled alternative to likelihood-maximization for building language models that are not only eloquent, but provably verifiable.

Chapter 1 — Sequential Conformal Coverage Under Adaptivity

1.1 Verifiability Process

Let (\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P}) be a probability space. A verifiability process is a tuple

\mathcal{M} = \big( \mathcal{C}, \{R_t\}_{t\ge 1}, V, \mathcal{O} \big)

with V:\mathcal{R}\times\mathcal{C}\to[0,1] measurable w.r.t. \sigma(\mathcal{O}), and \{R_t\} adapted to \{\mathcal{F}_t\}.

1.2 Weak Exchangeability Assumption

The calibration set D_{\text{cal}} = \{(C_i,R_i,\tilde{V}_i)\}_{i=1}^n and any test point (C,R,\tilde{V}) are exchangeable conditional on the model parameters \theta_0 at calibration time. If the model updates online, we adopt the rolling‑calibration scheme of Gibbs & Candès (2023, §4), which preserves conditional exchangeability.

1.3 Online Conformal p‑values and e‑values

For hypothesis H_t^0: V(R_t,C) \ge 1-\epsilon we compute a nonconformity score s_t = 1 - \hat{V}_t(R_t,C) and the conformal p‑value

p_t = \frac{1 + \sum_{i=1}^n \mathbf{1}\{s_i \ge s_t\}}{n+1}

using the calibration scores \{s_i\}. The e‑value for H_t^0 is

e_t = \frac{\mathbf{1}\{p_t \le \alpha\}}{\alpha}.

These e‑values are valid conditionally on D_{\text{cal}} and remain valid under arbitrary dependence among the test points.

1.4 Anytime‑Valid FDR Control

Theorem 1.1 (Anytime‑valid FDR control of verification failures).
Let \mathcal{R}_t be the set of hypotheses rejected (i.e., flagged as potentially incorrect) by the e‑BH procedure up to time t. Then for any adaptive sequence of responses,

\mathbb{P}\left( \sup_{t\ge 1} \frac{|\mathcal{R}_{0:t} \cap \mathcal{H}_0|}{|\mathcal{R}_{0:t}| \lor 1} \le \alpha \right) \ge 1-\delta,

where \mathcal{H}_0 is the set of true nulls. The complementary acceptance set \mathcal{A}_t = [t]\setminus\mathcal{R}_t enjoys the selective coverage guarantee

\mathbb{P}\left( \forall t, \frac{|\mathcal{A}_t \cap \{i: V_i \ge 1-\epsilon\}|}{|\mathcal{A}_t| \lor 1} \ge 1 - \alpha \right) \ge 1-\delta,

by the standard FDR‑FNR conversion of Benjamini & Yekutieli (2001).

Proof:
The e‑BH procedure of Wang & Ramdas (2022), applied to the stream of e‑values \{e_t\}, guarantees \text{FDR}\le\alpha simultaneously over any stopping time adapted to the common filtration \{\mathcal{F}_t\}. The conformal p‑values are valid conditionally, hence the e‑values are true e‑values; the supremum claim follows from the stopped e‑BH result of Ramdas et al. (2025). The FNR conversion is Lemma 2 in Benjamini & Yekutieli (2001) (FDR→FNR under independence; here it holds because the p‑values are conditionally independent given the calibration sample). ∎

Theorem 1.2 (Calibration sample complexity for risk control).
Let \ell(C,R) = \mathbf{1}\{V(R,C) < 1-\epsilon\} \in [0,1] be the loss of an accepted response. Using Conformal Risk Control (Angelopoulos et al., ICLR 2024), the empirical 1-\alpha quantile \hat{q}_\alpha of the calibration losses satisfies

\mathbb{E}_{\text{test}}[\ell(C,R)] \le \alpha + \varepsilon,\qquad \varepsilon = \sqrt{\frac{\log(2/\delta)}{2n}},

with probability \ge 1-\delta. Hence n = \Omega\big(\varepsilon^{-2}\log(1/\delta)\big) calibration samples suffice.

Proof:
Because \ell is bounded in [0,1], Hoeffding’s inequality directly yields the finite‑sample bound for the empirical quantile of the calibration losses; this is a standard application of Conformal Risk Control, see Angelopoulos et al. (2024, Theorem 1). ∎

