Chapter 2 — Semantic Epistemic Uncertainty as a Proper Verifiability Score

2.1 Kernel Variance with Bounded Embeddings

Let \phi:\mathcal{R}\to\mathcal{H} be a feature map into an RKHS with a characteristic kernel (e.g., RBF, for which \kappa = \sup_{R} \|\phi(R)\|_{\mathcal{H}}^2 = 1). The semantic variance of P(R\mid C) is

\text{Var}_{\phi}(P) = \mathbb{E}_{R\sim P}\big[ \|\phi(R) - \mu_P\|_{\mathcal{H}}^2 \big], \quad \mu_P = \mathbb{E}_{P}[\phi(R)].

2.2 Mean‑Square Lipschitz Verifiability

We do not require V to be globally Lipschitz. Instead we impose:

Assumption 2.1 (Local mean‑square Lipschitz).
Conditionally on context C,

\mathbb{E}\big[|V(R,C)-V(R',C)|^2 \mid C\big] \le L^2(C)\, \mathbb{E}\big[\|\phi(R)-\phi(R')\|_{\mathcal{H}}^2 \mid C\big],

for some L(C) that may depend on C. This holds, e.g., when V is a linear probe over a normalised embedding space.

2.3 Finite‑Sample Bound on Epistemic Uncertainty

The verifiability epistemic uncertainty is

U(C) = \mathbb{E}_{R\sim P(\cdot|C)}\big[ \big(V(R,C) - \mathbb{E}_{P}[V(R,C)]\big)^2 \big].

Theorem 2.1 (Finite‑sample bound).
Let \widehat{\text{Var}}_\phi be the U‑statistic estimate of \text{Var}_{\phi}(P) from M i.i.d. samples. Under Assumption 2.1, with probability at least 1-\delta,

U(C) \le L^2(C)\cdot \widehat{\text{Var}}_\phi
\;+\; L^2(C)\cdot \frac{2}{\sqrt{M}}\sqrt{2\log\frac{2}{\delta}}
\;+\; \text{MMD}(P,\widehat{P}_M),

where \text{MMD}(P,\widehat{P}_M) \le 2\sqrt{\frac{1}{M}} + \sqrt{\frac{2\log(2/\delta)}{M}} with probability 1-\delta (Gretton et al., 2012, Theorem 7).

Proof sketch: The first term uses the mean‑square Lipschitz property to bound U(C) by L^2(C)\,\text{Var}_{\phi}(P). The second is a Hoeffding bound for the U‑statistic of the kernel variance. The last term controls the deviation between the empirical and population distributions, which vanishes as M\to\infty. ∎

2.4 Proper Scoring Rule for Verification

Corollary 2.1 (Proper scoring rule).
The score

S(C) = -\log\!\big(1 - \min(1,\, L^2(C)\,\text{Var}_{\phi}(P) + \varepsilon_M)\big),

where \varepsilon_M = O(M^{-1/2}) is the concentration bound from Theorem 2.1, is a proper scoring rule for the event that a response will be verified correct. It is strictly proper iff the map P \mapsto \text{Var}_{\phi}(P) is injective on the considered family of distributions (holds for Gaussian embeddings).

Thus reducing the semantic variance directly minimises the epistemic uncertainty about verifiability, giving LMs a principled training objective.

