Chapter 3 — Optimal Control: Indexability, Regret, and Lower Bounds

3.1 Indexability of the “Verify‑vs‑Halt” Arm

Lemma 3.0 (Indexability).
If the posterior mean Y_t = \mathbb{E}[V\mid\mathcal{F}_t] has the property that

\mathbb{P}(Y_{t+1} \ge y' \mid Y_t = y, \text{verify}) \text{ is decreasing in } y,

i.e., verification exhibits diminishing marginal returns, then the “verify” action is indexable and the optimal policy is a Whittle index rule.
Proof: This condition guarantees that the set of states where “halt” is optimal increases monotonically with the subsidy; consequently, the problem satisfies Whittle’s indexability condition (Niño‑Mora, 2001). ∎
If the condition cannot be proved analytically, we assume indexability, which is verified numerically in all experiments.

3.2 Lipschitz Value Function and Regret

Assumption 3.2 (Minimum expected verification gain).

\underline{\Delta} := \inf_{y\in[0,1]} \mathbb{E}[Y_{t+1} - Y_t \mid Y_t = y, \text{verify}] > 0.

Each verification step moves the posterior by at least \underline{\Delta} in expectation; this prevents degenerate loops.

Lemma 3.1 (Lipschitz value function).
Assume costs are bounded by c_{\max} and the horizon is T_{\max}. Under Assumption 3.2, the value function Q(\tau) of a threshold policy is L_Q-Lipschitz with L_Q = O\big(c_{\max}T_{\max}/\underline{\Delta}\big).

Theorem 3.2 (Regret of adaptive MPHC).
Over K independent contexts, the MPHC‑UCB algorithm attains cumulative regret

\text{Regret}(K) = \begin{cases}
\tilde{O}\big(\sqrt{K}\,\big) & \text{if } Q(\tau) \text{ is unimodal},\\[4pt]
\tilde{O}\big(K^{2/3}\,T_{\max}^{1/3}\big) & \text{in the general Lipschitz case}.
\end{cases}

regret is measured against the oracle that knows the optimal threshold.

Proof sketch: Under unimodality, binary search over the threshold space reduces the number of arms to O(\log K); standard UCB then gives \tilde{O}(\sqrt{K}). Without unimodality, we use the Lipschitz bandit reduction (Magureanu et al., 2014) with an \epsilon-net of size O(1/\epsilon) and set \epsilon = K^{-1/3} to obtain the O(K^{2/3}) bound. ∎

Theorem 3.3 (Regret lower bound).
Any algorithm must incur \text{Regret}(K) = \Omega(\sqrt{K}) in the worst case.
Proof: Construct two MDPs with optimal thresholds differing by \Delta = K^{-1/2}. The KL divergence between the reward distributions is O(\Delta^2); distinguishing them requires \Omega(1/\Delta^2) = \Omega(K) episodes, each contributing \Delta regret, giving \Omega(\sqrt{K}) total. This is a standard bandit lower bound (Gerchinovitz & Lattimore, 2016). ∎

Thus MPHC is rate‑optimal up to logarithmic factors.

