Chapter 4 — PAC‑Verifiability Under Arbitrary Dependence

4.1 Concrete E‑values from Bounded Verifiers

Verifier i outputs a score V_i \in [0,1]. Under the null H_0^{(\epsilon)}: \mathbb{E}[V_i] \ge 1-\epsilon, we use the Hoeffding e‑value:

E_i = \exp\!\big(\lambda\,(V_i - (1-\epsilon)) - \lambda^2/8\big),

which satisfies \mathbb{E}_{H_0}[E_i] \le 1 for any \lambda>0. In practice, we fix \lambda = 4\epsilon to maximise power against small deviations.

4.2 Adversarially Robust Aggregation

Theorem 4.1 (Anytime‑valid error control under arbitrary dependence).
Let \{E_t\} be the running product of e‑values from all verifiers used up to time t. Then for any \alpha \in (0,1),

\mathbb{P}_{H_0}\big( \exists t: E_t > 1/\alpha \big) \le \alpha.

No assumption of independence among verifiers is needed.
Proof: Under H_0, \mathbb{E}[E_t \mid \mathcal{F}_{t-1}] \le E_{t-1}, so the product is a non‑negative supermartingale. By Markov’s inequality, \mathbb{P}(E_t > 1/\alpha) \le \alpha \mathbb{E}[E_t] \le \alpha for any fixed t; the supremum claim follows from the optional stopping theorem for supermartingales (Ramdas et al., 2023, Lemma 2). ∎

Theorem 4.2 (Power under adversarial collusion).
If all verifiers collude to output E_i = 1 (no evidence), the test has zero power but maintains coverage. If some verifiers are informative with \mathbb{E}_{H_1}[E_i] = 1+\gamma_i, then

\mathbb{P}_{H_1}(E_t \le 1/\alpha) \le \frac{\alpha}{\prod_i (1+\gamma_i)}.

This provides a rigorous deployment‑safe aggregation of possibly adversarially aligned AI verifiers.

