Chapter 5 — Human‑Criticality with Monotone Likelihood Ratios

5.1 Optimal Selective Verification

Define p_0(u) = \mathbb{P}(U=u \mid V \ge 1-\epsilon) and p_1(u) = \mathbb{P}(U=u \mid V < 1-\epsilon).

Assumption 5.1 (Monotone Likelihood Ratio).
The ratio p_1(u)/p_0(u) is non‑decreasing in u. This holds, for example, when higher uncertainty tightly correlates with lower verifiability.

Theorem 5.1 (Verifiability Neyman‑Pearson lemma).
The decision rule “route to human iff U > \tau_h” maximises the probability of automatic acceptance amongst all rules with the same human‑routed error rate.
Proof: Under MLR, the likelihood‑ratio test is monotone in U; the Neyman‑Pearson lemma (Lehmann & Romano, Theorem 3.4.1) then gives threshold optimality. ∎

5.2 Finite‑Sample Human Risk Control

Theorem 5.2 (Learn‑then‑Test bound).
Using a calibration set of size n, we can select \hat{\tau}_h such that with probability 1-\delta,

\mathbb{E}_{\text{test}}[\mathbf{1}\{U > \hat{\tau}_h\} \cdot (1-V(R))] \le r_0 + z_{1-\delta/2}\sqrt{\frac{\hat{r}(1-\hat{r})}{n}} + \frac{1}{2n},

where \hat{r} is the empirical human‑routed error rate on the calibration set.
Proof: This is the classical Wilson score interval, applied to the binary loss of human‑routed verifications; the term 1/(2n) is a continuity correction. ∎

