Chapter 6 — Information‑Theoretic Limits and Achievability

6.1 Verifier Channel Model

The verifier is modelled as a binary asymmetric channel:

\mathbb{P}(\tilde{V}=1 \mid V\ge 1-\epsilon) = 1-p,\qquad
\mathbb{P}(\tilde{V}=1 \mid V < 1-\epsilon) = p.

Its capacity is C = 1 - h(p), where h(p) is the binary entropy. For small p, C = \Theta(1/\Delta) with an appropriate parametrisation.

6.2 Converse

Theorem 6.1 (Minimum verification steps).
Any procedure achieving coverage 1-\alpha with confidence 1-\beta requires

T \ge \frac{H(R \mid C)}{C} \cdot \log\frac{1}{\alpha\beta}.

6.3 Achievability

Theorem 6.2 (Constructive upper bound).
Assume (\mathcal{R}, d) is a metric space with \epsilon-covering number N(\epsilon) \le (1/\epsilon)^d. Then a binary‑search‑like verification scheme achieves coverage 1-\alpha in at most

T \le d\,\frac{H(R \mid C)}{C} \log\frac{1}{\alpha} + O(1)

steps.

6.4 Rate‑Optimality of MPHC

Setting the computational cost \gamma = 0 and the cost per verification to c(\text{verify}) = 1/C, the MPHC policy from Chapter 3 halts after \Theta(H/C) steps in expectation, matching the lower bound of Theorem 6.1. Hence MPHC is rate‑optimal in terms of verification effort.

