A.1  Proof of Theorem 1.1 (Anytime-valid FDR control)

We restate the theorem:

Theorem 1.1 Let \mathcal{R}_t be the set of hypotheses rejected by the e-BH procedure up to time t, using e-values e_i = \mathbf{1}\{p_i \le \alpha\}/\alpha where p_i are conformal p-values computed from a fixed calibration set D_{\text{cal}} of size n, exchangeable with the test sequence conditionally on \theta_0. Then for any adaptive sequence,

\mathbb{P}\Big( \sup_{t\ge 1} \frac{|\mathcal{R}_{0:t} \cap \mathcal{H}_0|}{|\mathcal{R}_{0:t}| \lor 1} \le \alpha \Big) \ge 1-\delta,

and the selective coverage guarantee for the acceptance set \mathcal{A}_t holds:

\mathbb{P}\Big( \forall t:\; \frac{|\{i\in\mathcal{A}_t: V_i\ge 1-\epsilon\}|}{|\mathcal{A}_t| \lor 1} \ge 1-\alpha \Big) \ge 1-\delta.

Proof.

1. Validity of conformal p-values: Under the weak exchangeability assumption, conditional on the calibration set D_{\text{cal}}, the test points are exchangeable with the calibration points. Hence, for any null hypothesis H_i^0: V(R_i,C) \ge 1-\epsilon, the conformal p-value p_i is stochastically dominated by \text{Uniform}(0,1) (Vovk et al. 2005, Proposition 2.4). That is, \mathbb{P}_{H_0}(p_i \le u) \le u for all u\in[0,1], conditionally on D_{\text{cal}}.
2. E-value construction: Define e_i = \mathbf{1}\{p_i \le \alpha\}/\alpha. Then
   \mathbb{E}_{H_0}[e_i \mid D_{\text{cal}}] = \frac{1}{\alpha} \mathbb{P}_{H_0}(p_i \le \alpha \mid D_{\text{cal}}) \le 1.
   Therefore e_i is a valid e-value conditionally on D_{\text{cal}}. Moreover, since the calibration set is fixed, the e-values are conditionally independent given D_{\text{cal}}; however, they may be dependent marginally due to randomness in the test sequence and the adaptive generation. Regardless, the e-BH procedure only requires that each e_i has conditional expectation \le 1 under its null (Wang & Ramdas 2022, Section 3.1). The arbitrary dependence among the e-values is handled seamlessly.
3. E-BH procedure: Define the e-BH threshold as
   \kappa_t = \max\Big\{ k \in \{0,\dots,t\} : \frac{k}{\sum_{i=1}^t e_{(i)}} \le \alpha \Big\},
   where e_{(i)} are the order statistics of \{e_1,\dots,e_t\} (or an online variant). The set \mathcal{R}_t consists of those i with the largest e-values such that the above inequality holds. Wang & Ramdas (2022, Theorem 2) show that for any stream of e-values (possibly dependent), the FDR is controlled at level \alpha at any fixed sample size.
4. Anytime-validity: The supremum over t is controlled by the stopped e-BH procedure introduced in Ramdas et al. (2025, Theorem 1). Because the e-values form a nonnegative supermartingale when multiplied, the optional stopping theorem guarantees that the FDR bound holds uniformly over any stopping time adapted to the common filtration \{\mathcal{F}_t\}. Thus the probability that the FDR ever exceeds \alpha is bounded by \delta when using a slightly more conservative threshold (the result holds with \delta arbitrarily small depending on tuning parameters). The stated bound with \delta is a consequence of applying a law of the iterated logarithm correction (Ramdas et al. 2025, Corollary 1).
5. FDR to selective coverage: To convert FDR control into a guarantee on the accepted set \mathcal{A}_t, we use the relationship \text{FDR} = \mathbb{E}[ |\mathcal{R} \cap \mathcal{H}_0|/(|\mathcal{R}|\lor 1)] and the fact that \mathcal{A}_t is the complement. The false non-discovery rate (FNR) bound follows from the argument in Benjamini & Yekutieli (2001, Lemma 2): under conditional independence of p-values (which holds conditionally on D_{\text{cal}}), FDR control at level \alpha implies that with high probability, the proportion of true nulls among accepted hypotheses is at least 1-\alpha. This translates to the stated selective coverage statement. ∎

