A.2 Proof of Theorem 1.2 (Calibration sample complexity)

Proof. The conformal risk control framework (Angelopoulos et al. 2024) works as follows. Given a loss function \ell taking values in [0,1], a calibration set of size n, and a desired risk level \alpha, we choose a threshold \hat{\lambda} as the \lceil (n+1)(1-\alpha) \rceil-th smallest calibration loss. Then for any test point, the expected loss of the decision rule based on \hat{\lambda} is bounded by \alpha + \varepsilon with probability 1-\delta, where

\varepsilon = \sqrt{\frac{\log(2/\delta)}{2n}}

by Hoeffding's inequality (since the empirical quantile of bounded random variables concentrates around its population counterpart at this rate). Specifically, the DKW inequality for the empirical CDF gives the uniform convergence of the loss distribution, and the risk bound follows (Angelopoulos et al. 2024, Theorem 1). ∎

