G. Binary Search Verification for Information-Theoretic Achievability (Chapter 6)

For contexts where the answer space can be embedded into a metric space (e.g., mathematical proofs, code generation), we employ the constructive verification algorithm from Theorem 6.2.

```
Algorithm BinarySearchVerify(G, Embedder, Verifier, C, max_steps):
  Input: Generator G, embedder φ, binary verifier V_bin (outputs 1 if likely correct, 0 otherwise), context C
  Output: Verified response

  // Generate an initial candidate set via temperature sampling
  candidates ← G.sample_n(C, N_init)   // e.g., N_init = 10
  φ_candidates ← Embedder(candidates)

  // Build a Δ-net over the candidates; start with all.
  active_set ← {1..N_init}
  Δ ← 0.1  // initial resolution
  while |active_set| > 1 and steps < max_steps:
    // Pick a pivot: the candidate with highest average similarity to others (medoid)
    pivot_idx ← argmax_i (1/|active_set|) Σ_j∈active_set cos_sim(φ_i, φ_j)
    R_pivot ← candidates[pivot_idx]
    // Verify
    feedback ← Verifier(C, R_pivot)   // 1 if confident of correctness, else 0
    if feedback == 1:
      active_set ← {j in active_set : cos_sim(φ_j, φ_pivot) > threshold}
    else:
      active_set ← active_set \ {pivot_idx}
    Δ ← Δ / 2   // refine resolution
    steps++
  if active_set is empty: return best guess with highest verifier score so far
  else: return candidate corresponding to any remaining index (or majority vote)
```

The algorithm's step count scales as O(d \log(1/\Delta)) to isolate a correct answer, matching the information-theoretic upper bound.

