H. Implementation Notes

1. Embedding Model: Use a lightweight sentence transformer (e.g., MiniLM-L6-v2) for speed; for higher precision, a larger model may be used but caching embeddings saves compute.
2. Calibration Set Size: For the conformal FDR procedure (Chapter 1), n \approx 1000 gives \varepsilon \approx 0.03 (\delta=0.05). For the human threshold (Chapter 5), similar sample sizes yield tight bounds.
3. Hyperparameter Tuning:
   · M (sample size for variance): 20–50.
   · \lambda for e-value: fixed at 4\varepsilon (Chapter 4).
   · Discretization granularity for thresholds: 0.02 spacing (50 arms).
   · UCB exploration constant \sqrt{2} works well in practice.
4. Latency Budget: In a live system, the full loop (generate + verify + compute uncertainty + aggregate) should finish within 2–3 seconds. Parallelizing verifier calls and reusing embeddings greatly reduces wall-clock time.
5. Robustness: If any verifier returns a score outside [0,1], clip to the range. E-value product may be replaced by log-sum-exp to avoid overflow; compare logE_sum to log(1/α) for decision.

These algorithms constitute a production-ready instantiation of the Conformal Epistemic Process. All components have been implemented in Python (using PyTorch for embedding extraction) and benchmarked on standard NLG tasks, demonstrating the theoretical guarantees in practice.
